// Headscale ACL policy for allternit-headscale (headscale v0.29.2, HuJSON).
//
// ISOLATION MODEL
// ---------------
// One headscale user per customer (`clerk-<sanitized-clerk-id>`, created by
// allternit-cloud-api at enroll time). Every node enrolls under its owner's
// user via a preauth key and stays USER-OWNED (no ACL tags). The single rule
// below lets a node reach only nodes owned by the SAME headscale user —
// i.e. a customer's devices can reach each other and nothing else. ACLs are
// default-deny, so everything not listed here (cross-customer traffic,
// internet exit, subnet routes) is blocked. Subnet/exit routes additionally
// require manual approval (`headscale nodes approve-routes`); there are no
// autoApprovers, so none are ever approved automatically.
//
// WHY autogroup:self AND NOT PER-CUSTOMER TAGS (verified against the
// v0.29.2 source, hscontrol/policy/v2 + hscontrol/state/state.go):
//
//   * tagOwners keys are exact tag names — there is no wildcard support
//     (Tag.Validate / resolveTagOwners). A static file cannot enumerate
//     `tag:customer-<id>` for customers that do not exist yet.
//   * Tags in ACL src/dst also resolve per exact tag
//     (Tag.resolve matches node.HasTag), so per-tag ACL rules would need
//     the same impossible enumeration.
//   * autogroup:self destinations REJECT tag and autogroup:tagged sources
//     (validateACLSrcDstCombination → ErrACLAutogroupSelfInvalidSource),
//     and compileAutogroupSelf returns no rules for tagged nodes. So once
//     a node is tagged, the only dynamic same-owner rule is unavailable.
//   * The only static rules expressible for tagged nodes are per-tag
//     (enumeration, impossible) or autogroup:tagged → autogroup:tagged
//     (every customer reaches every customer — no isolation at all).
//
// Conclusion: with a static policy file, per-customer isolation is only
// expressible via user ownership + autogroup:self. The alternative —
// policy.mode: database with cloud-api appending per-customer
// tagOwners/ACL rules over gRPC SetPolicy on every enroll — was rejected:
// it mutates shared tailnet state from the request path, tagged nodes lose
// their user association (worse ops visibility), and it buys nothing over
// the model above.
//
// Side effect worth knowing: SetNodeTags / client --advertise-tags require
// the tag to exist in tagOwners (polMan.TagExists). With no tagOwners
// declared, no node can ever be tagged, so the isolation model cannot be
// escaped by tagging.
{
  "acls": [
    // A node's traffic may go only to nodes owned by the same headscale
    // user (= the same customer), on any port/protocol.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["autogroup:self:*"],
    },
  ],
}
